The patch changes the zero case in `reciprocal()` from returning `NaN` to returning `INF`, which is the behavior the failing test expects.

More importantly, for the reachable inputs of this method, the added condition is effectively redundant:

```java
if (real == 0.0 && imaginary == 0.0) {
    if (this.equals(new Complex(0,0))){return INF;}
    return NaN;
}
```

Given the class’s own `equals` semantics for `Complex` values, any instance that satisfies `real == 0.0 && imaginary == 0.0` will also compare equal to `new Complex(0,0)`, so this branch will return `INF` for zero inputs. That is equivalent in effect to replacing `return NaN;` with `return INF;`.

I do not see a remaining sibling input in `reciprocal()` that would still produce the old wrong value from the same root cause. Nonzero finite values still use the normal reciprocal computation, `NaN` still returns `NaN`, and infinite values still return `ZERO`, matching the surrounding tests.

The patch is ugly and unnecessarily indirect, but it does fix the zero-reciprocal behavior rather than merely matching one exact object identity pattern.

